  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio (Next.js 16 / React 19)\n\nThis is a static marketing site with no database, API routes, or data-fetching layer — several of the requested categories don't apply in their usual form, but I verified the actual files (not just the truncated excerpts) and found concrete, fixable issues. Findings below are grounded in the current code, not assumptions.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — not applicable\nNo `app/api`, ORM, `fetch`, or `getServerSideProps`/`generateStaticParams` calls exist anywhere in the codebase. `TEAM`, `PRODUCTS`, `PRINCIPLES`, `LINKS`, `QUOTES` are all hardcoded arrays. Nothing to fix here today — flag it for a future review once the \"Insights\" content or team roster moves to a CMS/API, since that's exactly when a `.map()` over items with a per-item `fetch` would silently become an N+1.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders\n\n**a) `Products.tsx:199-244` — hovering one row re-renders all five rows**\n`activeIndex` lives in the parent `Products`, and each `ProductRow` gets brand-new `onActivate`/`onDeactivate` closures every render:\n```tsx\nonActivate={() => activate(i)}\nonDeactivate={() => setActiveIndex(null)}\n```\nEven wrapping `ProductRow` in `React.memo` wouldn't help as-is, since these inline functions are new references on every parent render. Fix by stabilizing the callbacks and letting each row supply its own index:\n```tsx\nconst ProductRow = memo(function ProductRow({ product, index, active, onActivate, onDeactivate }: Props) {\n  ...\n  onMouseEnter={() => onActivate(index)}\n  onMouseLeave={onDeactivate}\n});\n\n// Products()\nconst activate = useCallback((i: number) => { setActiveIndex(i); setPreviewIndex(i); }, []);\nconst deactivate = useCallback(() => setActiveIndex(null), []);\n```\nNow only the two rows whose `active` boolean actually flips re-render, instead of all five.\n\n**b) `Nav.tsx:25-30` — scroll listener dispatches state on every scroll tick**\n```tsx\nconst onScroll = () => setScrolled(window.scrollY > 48);\nwindow.addEventListener(\"scroll\", onScroll, { passive: true });\n```\nReact bails the re-render when the boolean is unchanged, so this isn't causing extra paints, but it's a raw `window` scroll listener running independently of the Lenis RAF loop already driving `SmoothScroll.tsx`. Prefer hooking into Lenis's own `scroll` event (or throttling via `requestAnimationFrame`) so scroll-tracking doesn't run twice per frame:\n```tsx\n// if Lenis instance is lifted to context, or via lenis.on(\"scroll\", ({ scroll }) => setScrolled(scroll > 48))\n```\n\n**c) `Preloader.tsx:24-40` — `setProgress` fires every animation frame for ~1.5s**\nThis is intentional (a visible counter) and scoped to the preloader only, not a bug — just noting it's the app's single heaviest render loop by frame count.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- `next.config.ts` is empty (`{ /* config options here */ }`). Since `AboutPage.tsx` already uses `next/image` correctly (`fill`, tuned `sizes`), no urgent change is needed, but neither the founder photo nor the first team row sets `priority`, so if either is your About-page LCP element it will lazy-load unnecessarily. Add `priority` to the founder `<Image>` at `AboutPage.tsx:259-265`.\n- `CTA.tsx:8-16` already does the right thing — `TICKS` is precomputed once at module scope with a comment explaining it's for SSR/CSR hydration parity. `Trust.tsx`'s `ticks()` and `AboutPage.tsx`'s `CompassSeal` should follow the same pattern (see redundant computations below) — that hoist is effectively a caching fix.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\n**Real bug — `Preloader.tsx:36-46`: uncanceled `setTimeout` survives unmount**\n```tsx\n} else {\n  window.setTimeout(() => {\n    if (!doneRef.current) { doneRef.current = true; onDone(); }\n    setVisible(false);\n    document.documentElement.style.overflow = \"\";\n  }, HOLD_MS);\n}\n...\nreturn () => {\n  cancelAnimationFrame(raf);          // only this is cleaned up\n  document.documentElement.style.overflow = \"\";\n};\n```\nThe cleanup function only cancels the `raf`; it never captures/clears the nested `setTimeout`. If the component unmounts during the 280ms `HOLD_MS` window (e.g. rapid navigation away right as loading finishes), the timeout still fires later, calling `onDone()`/`setVisible` on an unmounted tree and mutating `document.documentElement.style.overflow` after nothing owns it anymore. Fix:\n```tsx\nlet hold: number | undefined;\nconst tick = (now: number) => {\n  ...\n  if (t < 1) {\n    raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n  } else {\n    hold = window.setTimeout(() => { ... }, HOLD_MS);\n  }\n};\nraf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\nreturn () => {\n  cancelAnimationFrame(raf);\n  window.clearTimeout(hold);\n  document.documentElement.style.overflow = \"\";\n};\n```\nEverything else checked out clean: `SmoothScroll.tsx` cancels its RAF and calls `lenis.destroy()`; `MarketConstellation.tsx`'s `Terrain` explicitly disposes its custom `BufferGeometry`; R3F auto-disposes JSX-declared materials/geometries on unmount; `Nav.tsx`'s scroll/overflow effects both return proper cleanups.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n**a) `AboutPage.tsx:82-97` (`CompassSeal`) and `Trust.tsx:8-21`/`24-52` (`ticks()`, `AmfiSeal`, `SebiSeal`) recompute SVG tick geometry on every render**, even though the geometry is a pure function of `variant`, which only has two possible values. `CTA.tsx` already solves this correctly by precomputing `TICKS` once at module scope. Apply the same pattern:\n```tsx\nconst buildTicks = (count: number, inner: number) => Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => { ... });\nconst AMFI_TICKS = buildTicks(24, 23.5);\nconst SEBI_TICKS = buildTicks(8, 17);\n\nfunction CompassSeal({ variant }: { variant: \"amfi\" | \"sebi\" }) {\n  const ticks = variant === \"amfi\" ? AMFI_TICKS : SEBI_TICKS;\n  ...\n}\n```\nZero behavior change, removes trig work from the render path entirely.\n\n**b) `Reveal.tsx:46` (`RevealWords`) and `Manifesto.tsx:36`** call `text.split(\" \")` / `STATEMENT.split(\" \")` fresh on every render. Low-impact given current render frequency, but trivial to fix — `Manifesto.tsx` can hoist `const WORDS = STATEMENT.split(\" \")` to module scope; `RevealWords` can wrap in `useMemo(() => text.split(\" \"), [text])` since `text` is always a stable literal.\n\n### Priority ranking\n1. Fix the `Preloader.tsx` timeout leak (real bug, cheap fix).\n2. Memoize `Products.tsx`'s row callbacks (visible re-render cascade on every hover).\n3. Hoist `CompassSeal`/`Trust.tsx` tick arrays to module scope (matches the pattern `CTA.tsx` already established).\n4. Everything else (Nav scroll listener, `RevealWords`/`Manifesto` splits) is minor polish, not worth prioritizing over the above.\n",
